# AASA — Chroma vector store (Card 2.2).
#
# chroma_store/ IS COMMITTED as of 2026-07-28 (Build Guide 36) and the ignore rule
# below is deliberately disabled, not accidentally broken. Streamlit Community
# Cloud only gets what is in the repo, and the store cannot be rebuilt there
# because data/use-cases.csv is excluded (see the next block) — so shipping the
# pre-built store is what makes the deployed app work at all.
#
# Two consequences to keep in view rather than rediscover:
#   1. It is ~52 MB of binary, over GitHub's 50 MB advisory threshold. Pushes
#      succeed but print a Git LFS suggestion. Harmless.
#   2. It is NOT just vectors — chunk metadata carries the source dataset's
#      title, organization, outcomes and ~3.1 MB of verbatim case prose. So
#      committing it *is* a redistribution of the upstream data in a different
#      container, which is permitted under MIT with attribution. Attribution
#      lives in docs/data-attribution.md.
# chroma_store/
data/use_cases_chunks.jsonl

# AASA — third-party dataset (MIT-licensed, not our work): the RAW CSV is not
# committed. Download your own copy — see "Before you start" in
# 13-Build-Guide-Epic2-Retrieval-v1.md for the source repo, licence and citation.
#
# Honest note on what this rule does and doesn't achieve: since 2026-07-28 the
# derived chroma_store/ IS committed and contains the same case text in its chunk
# metadata. So this rule no longer means "the upstream data isn't distributed via
# this repo" — it means the canonical CSV isn't, and people building the store
# themselves fetch it from source. Attribution: docs/data-attribution.md.
data/use-cases.csv

# macOS
.DS_Store

# AASA — slide decks are deliberately NOT ignored.
#
# Cleaned up 2026-07-30 (P.22). This block used to ignore PM_slides/ and
# AASA_Week2_Stakeholder_Checkin.pptx — neither of which has existed in this repo
# for weeks — and it also listed .python-version, which IS tracked. That is the
# same ignored-yet-tracked contradiction we fixed for data/telemetry.log: git
# keeps honouring the tracked copy, so the rule does nothing except mislead the
# next person reading this file. All three removed.
#
# The final decks were removed from the branch on 2026-07-28 while wording is
# finalised and will be re-added by hand, so do NOT add a *.pptx rule here — it
# would silently swallow them on the way back in.

# AASA — data/telemetry.log is deliberately NOT ignored (force-tracked).
#
# It was ignored-yet-tracked for most of the project, then untracked on
# 2026-07-28 to resolve that contradiction. That resolved it the wrong way: the
# log is the sole evidence behind every Card P.14 validation figure quoted in the
# write-up and the pitch deck, and scripts/telemetry_funnel.py and
# scripts/credible_interval.py read it directly. Untracked, those scripts raise
# FileNotFoundError for anyone but the machine that produced the data, and no
# published number can be reproduced or audited.
#
# It contains no personal data by design (Card P.4): event names, timestamps and
# numeric/enum values only, no user identifier and no free text. Keeping it in the
# repo is therefore a deliberate reproducibility decision, not an oversight — do
# not re-add an ignore rule for it without moving the evidence somewhere else
# first.

# Real-user testing: the unredacted session sheet holds participants' names, job
# titles and employers. They consented to testing, not to being named in a
# submitted document, so only the pseudonymised copy (P1-P8, employers withheld)
# is committed. Keep the original locally.
PM & Ethics/AI-Assisted Stack Architect User Testing.xlsx
